          <button class="tool" type="button" id="md-split">Split by hand…</button>
          <button class="tool" type="button" id="md-none">Select none</button>
          <button class="tool" type="button" id="md-anim"
                  aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="anim-box">Animate…</button>
          <button class="tool" type="button" id="md-clear">Clear all</button>
          <button class="tool" type="button" id="md-grid-x" aria-pressed="true">X grid</button>
          <button class="tool" type="button" id="md-grid-y" aria-pressed="false">Y grid</button>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="pattern-book" id="pattern-book" hidden>
        <div class="book-head">
          <label for="pb-search">Search the objects</label>
          <input id="pb-search" type="search" autocomplete="off" spellcheck="false"
                 placeholder="tree, column, chalice, gear&hellip;">
          <span class="muted xsmall" id="pb-count"></span>
        </div>
        <div class="book-shelf" id="pb-shelf"></div>
      </div>


      <div class="anim-box" id="anim-box" hidden>
        <label for="md-anim-text">How would you like it to move?</label>
        <textarea id="md-anim-text" rows="2" spellcheck="false"
                  placeholder="Spin it slowly anticlockwise, and let it bob in the air."></textarea>
        <div class="anim-actions">
          <button class="btn btn--small" type="button" id="md-anim-go">Set it moving</button>
          <button class="btn btn--small btn--ghost" type="button" id="md-anim-stop">Hold still</button>
        </div>
        <p class="muted xsmall" id="md-anim-read">Plain words are enough: spin, turn, orbit, bob, float,
        swing, rock, pulse, breathe, drift, wobble, bounce — with slowly, quickly, gently, wildly,
        clockwise or anticlockwise, and end over end for a tumble. Whatever is selected takes the
        instruction, so several pieces can be set going at once.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="texture-box split-box" id="split-box" hidden>
        <h4>The splitting bench</h4>
        <p class="muted xsmall">The piece on its own, taken to bits. Every part of it belongs to one of
        the numbered pieces-to-be: choose a piece-to-be below, then click parts in the window — or
        their rows in the list — to put them in it. Drag the window to walk round, scroll to come
        closer. The colours are only there to show you what goes where; the real colours come back when
        the cut is made.</p>

        <div class="split-cols">
          <div class="split-stage" id="split-stage" role="img"
               aria-label="The piece being taken apart, part by part"></div>
          <div>
            <div class="split-buckets" id="split-buckets"></div>
            <div class="split-list" id="split-parts"></div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="ask-actions">
          <button class="btn" type="button" id="split-do">Make the cut</button>
          <button class="btn btn--ghost" type="button" id="split-auto">Read it for me again</button>
          <button class="btn btn--ghost" type="button" id="split-close">Leave it whole</button>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="texture-box" id="texture-box" hidden>
        <h4>The texture studio</h4>
        <p class="muted xsmall">A picture of your own laid onto the piece you are holding. Nothing is
        uploaded — the file is read straight into the page. Choose which surfaces wear it, then set
        where it sits, which way up it is and how big it is against the shape.</p>

        <div class="tx-head">
          <div class="tx-pics" id="tx-list"></div>
          <button class="tool" type="button" id="tx-import">Bring in another</button>
        </div>

        <div class="tx-cols">
          <div>
            <h5>Which surface</h5>
            <div class="tx-surfaces" id="tx-surfaces"></div>
          </div>
          <div>
            <h5>Where, which way, how big</h5>
            <div class="model-slider">
              <label for="tx-x">Across</label>
              <input id="tx-x" type="range" min="-1" max="1" step="0.01" value="0">
            </div>
            <div class="model-slider">
              <label for="tx-y">Up the surface</label>
              <input id="tx-y" type="range" min="-1" max="1" step="0.01" value="0">
            </div>
            <div class="model-slider">
              <label for="tx-rot">Turn</label>
              <input id="tx-rot" type="range" min="0" max="360" step="1" value="0">
            </div>
            <div class="model-slider">
              <label for="tx-sw">Width</label>
              <input id="tx-sw" type="range" min="0.05" max="4" step="0.05" value="1">
            </div>
            <div class="model-slider">
              <label for="tx-sh">Height</label>
              <input id="tx-sh" type="range" min="0.05" max="4" step="0.05" value="1">
            </div>
            <p class="byok-remember">
              <label class="check">
                <input id="tx-tile" type="checkbox">
                <span>Repeat it across the surface rather than placing it once</span>
              </label>
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="ask-actions">
          <button class="btn" type="button" id="tx-apply">Lay it on</button>
          <button class="btn btn--ghost" type="button" id="tx-strip">Take it off</button>
          <button class="btn btn--ghost" type="button" id="tx-close">Close the studio</button>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="model-sliders" id="model-sliders" hidden>
        <div class="model-slider">
          <label for="md-x">Left &amp; right</label>
          <input id="md-x" type="range" min="-6" max="6" step="0.05" value="0">
        </div>
        <div class="model-slider">
          <label for="md-y">Up &amp; down</label>
          <input id="md-y" type="range" min="-3" max="8" step="0.05" value="0">
        </div>
        <div class="model-slider">
          <label for="md-z">Near &amp; far</label>
          <input id="md-z" type="range" min="-6" max="6" step="0.05" value="0">
        </div>
        <div class="model-slider">
          <label for="md-scale">Size</label>
          <input id="md-scale" type="range" min="0.1" max="4" step="0.02" value="1">
        </div>
        <div class="model-slider">
          <label for="md-turn">Turn</label>
          <input id="md-turn" type="range" min="0" max="360" step="1" value="0">
        </div>
        <div class="model-slider">
          <label for="md-tilt">Tilt</label>
          <input id="md-tilt" type="range" min="-180" max="180" step="1" value="0">
        </div>
      </div>

      <p class="muted xsmall" id="md-status">Add a solid to begin. Pieces arrive at a fifth of their
      size, so grow them with the Size slider.</p>

      <p class="muted xsmall">With a piece held, the left button works the piece and not the camera:
      <strong>drag</strong> slides it across the floor, <strong>Ctrl&nbsp;+&nbsp;drag</strong> lifts and
      lowers it in the upright plane, <strong>Alt&nbsp;+&nbsp;drag</strong> turns it in the floor plane
      and <strong>Ctrl&nbsp;+&nbsp;Alt</strong> tips it end over end. Holding <strong>both buttons</strong>, or
      <strong>Ctrl&nbsp;+&nbsp;Shift</strong>, resizes it from its own centre. The arrow keys repeat
      all of them in steps. <strong>Tab</strong> walks the selection on to the next piece,
      or round the handful you are holding, and Shift+Tab goes back. Two or more pieces can be
      <strong>stitched together</strong> into one object. <strong>Unstitch</strong> takes a piece
      apart again: a stitched piece comes apart at its seams, and anything else is read for its natural
      divisions — a tree falls into trunk and leaves rather than into nine loose spheres.
      <strong>Split by hand</strong> opens a second little window where you say exactly where the cuts
      go.
      For the camera: <strong>right-drag</strong>, or Shift, or an empty selection turns the view;
      <strong>Alt&nbsp;+&nbsp;Shift</strong> walks it across the floor,
      <strong>Ctrl&nbsp;+&nbsp;Alt&nbsp;+&nbsp;Shift</strong> or the <strong>wheel</strong> walks it up
      and down, and <strong>Ctrl&nbsp;+&nbsp;wheel</strong> comes closer. Shift-click picks up more than one piece, and <strong>Lasso several</strong>
      draws a box round a whole handful at once;
      <strong>Ctrl&nbsp;+&nbsp;Z</strong> and <strong>Ctrl&nbsp;+&nbsp;Y</strong> undo and redo.</p>

      <div class="ask-actions">
        <button class="btn" type="button" id="md-export">Export to a file</button>
        <button class="btn" type="button" id="md-to-image">Put this model on the sketch pad</button>
        <button class="btn" type="button" id="md-to-film">Send it to the video generator</button>
        <button class="btn btn--ghost" type="button" id="md-render">Attach the whole view as a sketch</button>
      </div>
      <div class="ask-actions">
        <button class="btn btn--ghost" type="button" id="md-words-image">Describe it to the image
        generator</button>
        <button class="btn btn--ghost" type="button" id="md-words-film">Describe it to the video
        generator</button>
        <button class="btn btn--ghost" type="button" id="md-vectors-image">Send exact vectors to the
        image generator</button>
        <button class="btn btn--ghost" type="button" id="md-vectors-film">Send exact vectors to the
        video generator</button>
      </div>
      <p class="auth-msg" id="md-msg" role="status"></p>

      <p class="muted xsmall">The OBJ file carries the shapes and their positions; colours are written
      alongside it in the same download as an MTL. <strong>Put this model on the sketch pad</strong>
      cuts the pieces out of their background and hands them to the sketch pad above: the pad opens,
      the cut-out follows the cursor, the scroll wheel sizes it and the next click prints it where you
      want it, so the model becomes one element of a larger drawing.
      <strong>Send it to the video generator</strong> hands the view down to the film box as the
      clip&rsquo;s literal opening frame, so the camera starts on the arrangement you built.
      Attaching the whole view instead gives the image generator the entire window, grid and all,
      as a single sketch.</p>

      <p class="muted xsmall">The four buttons above write the bench out as words instead of as a
      picture. <strong>Describe it</strong> gives a model that answers to English the colours,
      materials, sizes and where everything stands; <strong>exact vectors</strong> gives one that
      answers to numbers every piece’s centre, turn, scale, bounds and colour, with the camera
      written out at the end. Both are <em>added</em> to whatever is already in that prompt box —
      nothing you have written is ever overwritten — joined on with a single full stop and a
      space.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="draw-box montage-box reveal" id="montage-box">
      <p class="kicker">THE CUTTING ROOM</p>
      <h3>Make a Montage</h3>
      <p class="muted small">Every clip <em>and every picture</em> you generate on this page is collected
      here. Put them in order, trim the ends or set how long a still holds, choose a cut or a crossfade, lay sound
      under the whole thing or onto single scenes,
      and the page will edit them into one film — rendered in
      your own browser, with no upload and no service in the middle. The result downloads as a single
      file. Clips made before you cleared the page are not kept; this is a cutting room, not an archive.</p>

      <ul class="shot-list" id="shot-list" aria-live="polite">
        <li class="shot-empty">No shots yet. Draw a picture or film a clip above and it lands here — then any shot can be continued from, so the next one keeps the same characters.</li>
      </ul>

      <div class="film-row">
        <div>
          <label for="m-transition">Between shots</label>
          <select id="m-transition" name="transition">
            <option value="cut">Straight cut</option>
            <option value="0.5">Crossfade · half a second</option>
            <option value="1">Crossfade · one second</option>
          </select>
        </div>
        <div>
          <label for="m-shape">Shape</label>
          <select id="m-shape" name="shape">
            <option value="854x480">Landscape · 854 × 480</option>
            <option value="480x854">Upright · 480 × 854</option>
          </select>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="montage-sound">
        <h4>Sound</h4>
        <p class="muted xsmall">The models make no sound at all, so every clip here is silent. Bring
        your own: a bed of music or narration under the whole film, or a piece of audio dropped onto
        one scene or a run of scenes. Scene sound wins wherever it lands — the bed drops out
        underneath it for exactly that stretch and comes back after. Nothing is uploaded; the audio is
        decoded and mixed in this page while the cut plays.</p>

        <div class="sound-row">
          <label class="btn btn--ghost btn--small" for="m-bed-file">Sound under the whole film</label>
          <input id="m-bed-file" type="file" accept="audio/*" class="sound-file">
          <button class="btn btn--ghost btn--small" type="button" id="m-shot-sound" disabled>Sound on the
          selected scenes</button>
          <input id="m-shot-file" type="file" accept="audio/*" class="sound-file">
        </div>
        <div class="sound-bed" id="m-bed" hidden></div>
      </div>

      <div class="ask-actions">
        <button class="btn" type="button" id="m-render" disabled>Edit the montage</button>
        <button class="btn btn--ghost" type="button" id="m-clear" hidden>Empty the room</button>
      </div>
      <p class="auth-msg" id="m-msg" role="status"></p>
      <div class="montage-out" id="montage-out"></div>

      <p class="muted xsmall">The edit happens frame by frame on a canvas in this page and is recorded as
      it plays, so a two-minute montage takes two minutes to make. Nothing is sent anywhere. For anything
      more ambitious — narration, music, titles, stock footage, a real timeline —
      <a href="https://github.com/calesthio/OpenMontage" rel="noopener">OpenMontage</a> is installed in
      this repository under <code>openmontage/</code>; see the README there.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="byok" id="mind-byok">
      <button class="btn btn--ghost" type="button" id="mk-open"
              aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="mbyok-box">Lend the oracle a mind</button>
      <div class="byok-box" id="mbyok-box" hidden>
        <p class="muted xsmall">The order writes its own answers first and reaches for a model only when
        nothing it has written fits. As of October 2026 there is no longer a single free hosted model that
        will answer an unkeyed request from this server &mdash; we tested seven and published the results.
        So: if you hold a key, lend it for the length of one question. <strong>Google AI Studio, Groq and
        OpenRouter all give one away free in about two minutes</strong>, and any of the three is a good deal
        better than the keyless routes ever were. Used once, never written down, never logged, and the
        written answers still come first.</p>

        <div class="byok-grid">
          <div>
            <label for="mk-provider">Provider</label>
            <select id="mk-provider">
              <option value="google">Google AI Studio &middot; free</option>
              <option value="groq">Groq &middot; free</option>
              <option value="openrouter">OpenRouter &middot; free tier</option>
              <option value="cerebras">Cerebras</option>
              <option value="mistral">Mistral</option>
              <option value="openai">OpenAI</option>
              <option value="custom">Any other service</option>
            </select>
          </div>
          <div>
            <label for="mk-model">Model</label>
            <input id="mk-model" type="text" autocomplete="off" spellcheck="false"
                   placeholder="gemini-2.0-flash">
          </div>
          <div>
            <label for="mk-key">Your key</label>
            <input id="mk-key" type="password" autocomplete="current-password"
                   spellcheck="false" placeholder="AIza&hellip; or gsk_&hellip;">
          </div>
          <div id="mk-base-wrap" hidden>
            <label for="mk-base">The chat endpoint</label>
            <input id="mk-base" type="url" autocomplete="off" spellcheck="false"
                   placeholder="https://api.example.com/v1/chat/completions">
          </div>
        </div>

        <p class="byok-remember">
          <label class="check">
            <input id="mk-keep" type="checkbox">
            <span>Keep it in this tab until I close it</span>
          </label>
        </p>

        <div class="ask-actions">
          <button class="btn" type="button" id="mk-use">Use this key</button>
          <button class="btn btn--ghost" type="button" id="mk-forget">Forget it</button>
        </div>
        <p class="auth-msg" id="mk-msg" role="status"></p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <p class="muted xsmall oracle-foot">Test everything kindly. If an answer cannot survive your scepticism,
    it does not deserve your belief — and the written form below reaches the dragon himself.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<div class="divider">✦ ✦ ✦</div>

{#- The order's own door on this page. Open it and two things appear: the
    five Free.ai doors, and an "export to the live files" button under every
    generation on the page. Nothing here is visible to a visitor. -#}
<section class="wrap narrow admin-media" id="ask-admin">
  <div class="btn-row">
    <button type="button" class="btn btn--ghost" data-aa="open" aria-expanded="false">Admin Only</button>
    <span class="muted xsmall">The order&rsquo;s own models, and a way to send what ShakDrah makes
    into the live files folder.</span>
  </div>
  <div class="frame admin-panel" data-aa="panel" hidden>
    <p class="kicker">The Order&rsquo;s Passcode</p>
    <div class="admin-signin">
      <label for="ask-admin-pass">Passcode</label>
      <input type="password" id="ask-admin-pass" data-aa="pass" autocomplete="current-password"
             placeholder="&bull;&bull;&bull;&bull;&bull;">
      <button type="button" class="btn btn--small" data-aa="signin">Open the door</button>
    </div>
    <p class="auth-msg" data-aa="msg" role="status"></p>
    {% set faWhere = "ask" %}
    {% include "partials/freeai-doors.njk" %}
  </div>
</section>

<div class="divider">✦</div>

<section class="wrap">
  <div class="grid two">
    <div class="frame reveal">
      <h3 style="margin-bottom:1rem">Write to the dragon directly</h3>
      <form class="ornate" id="letter-form">
        <div>
          <label for="name">Your name or chosen name</label>
          <input id="name" name="name" type="text" required placeholder="Rowan of the North">
        </div>
        <div>
          <label for="email">Email (so the dragon can reply)</label>
          <input id="email" name="email" type="email" required placeholder="you@example.com">
        </div>
        <div>
          <label for="topic">Which limb does this belong to?</label>
          <select id="topic" name="topic">
            <option>Cosmic physics &amp; astronomy</option>
            <option>Druidry, trees &amp; nature</option>
            <option>Sacred geometry</option>
            <option>Astrology</option>
            <option>Neuroscience &amp; psychology</option>
            <option>Biology &amp; life</option>
            <option>God, the Law of One &amp; contact</option>
            <option>Magic &amp; the wizard's craft</option>
            <option>History, propaganda &amp; the shadow</option>
            <option>Something that fits nowhere</option>
          </select>
        </div>
        <div>
          <label for="question">Your question</label>
          <textarea id="question" name="question" required placeholder="Ask plainly. the dragon would rather have the real question than the polite one."></textarea>
        </div>
        <div>
          <label for="public">May we answer this publicly?</label>
          <select id="public" name="public">
            <option>Yes — use my name</option>
            <option>Yes — but keep me anonymous</option>
            <option>No — private reply only</option>
          </select>
        </div>
        <div class="hp" aria-hidden="true">
          <label for="website">Leave this empty</label>
          <input id="website" name="website" type="text" tabindex="-1" autocomplete="off">
        </div>
        <div class="captcha" data-captcha="letter">
          <p class="captcha-label">Read the gate-word and type it back</p>
          <div class="captcha-row">
            <div class="captcha-art" data-captcha-art aria-live="polite"></div>
            <button class="captcha-new" type="button" data-captcha-new
                    title="Draw another word" aria-label="Draw another gate-word">&#8634;</button>
          </div>
          <input type="text" data-captcha-input autocomplete="off" autocapitalize="characters"
                 spellcheck="false" maxlength="8" required
                 aria-label="The gate-word" placeholder="The five letters above">
        </div>
        <button class="btn" type="submit" id="letter-send">Send the Question</button>
        <p class="auth-msg" id="letter-msg" role="status"></p>
        <p class="form-note">Your letter is stored, forwarded to Ed, and acknowledged by return of post —
        the acknowledgement arrives within a minute and carries a reference you can quote. If nothing
        appears, check the spam fold, or write to
        <a href="mailto:{{ site.email }}">{{ site.email }}</a> directly.</p>
      </form>
    </div>

    <div class="reveal">
      <div class="frame mb-2">
        <h2>How the dragon Answers</h2>
        <ul class="biblio">
          <li>Every question is read. Most are answered within a fortnight.</li>
          <li>The best are answered at length in the <a href="/podcast/">podcast mailbag</a>.</li>
          <li>the dragon will tell you plainly when he does not know — that is the most common answer, and the most useful.</li>
          <li>Where evidence exists, you get the evidence. Where only tradition exists, you get told it is tradition.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>

      <div class="frame caution">
        <p><strong>Please read.</strong> EGregoRA is a study order, not a clinic. the dragon cannot give medical,
        psychiatric, legal or financial advice, and will not advise on taking any controlled substance. If you
        are in crisis, please contact your local emergency service or a crisis line
        (UK: Samaritans, 116 123, free, 24 hours) rather than waiting for a reply here.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="frame mt-2">
        <h3>Questions Already in the Pile</h3>
        <details class="scroll"><summary>Is astrology real?</summary>
          <p>Real as a symbolic system with four millennia of continuous use and enormous cultural weight — yes,
          demonstrably. Real as a physical force by which Saturn's position alters your week — no; every
          controlled test of that claim has failed. Both halves of that sentence matter.</p></details>
        <details class="scroll"><summary>Does the Law of One hold up?</summary>
          <p>As cosmology, it is untestable. As philosophy, its core — that consciousness is unitary and that free
          will is sacred — sits in good company with Advaita Vedanta, Plotinus and Whitehead. Read it as a text, not
          a transcript, and it becomes much more interesting.</p></details>
      </div>

      <div class="frame mt-2 oracle-disclaimer">
        <p class="kicker">What &ldquo;The Order&rsquo;s view&rdquo; means, and does not</p>
        <p>The Order will give you its view on anything you ask. That is a promise about
        <em>willingness</em>, not about authority. What answers you is an oracle built by this
        order: a body of written answers composed by hand, and, where none of them fits, a language
        model asked to reason in the order&rsquo;s voice. It is not Edward Gregory, it is not Jim
        Rankin, and a view is not a verdict.</p>
        <p>So: it can be <strong>wrong</strong>, and confidently so. It can misremember a date, a
        dose, a citation or a law. Where it has looked something up it shows you where it went, and
        those sources deserve the same scepticism as everything else here. On anything that touches
        your body, your mind, your money, your safety or the law, treat what you read as a place to
        <em>start</em> thinking, and take it to a doctor, a lawyer, an accountant or someone who
        knows you. It does not know your history, cannot see you, and will not notice if you are in
        trouble.</p>
        <p>If you are in crisis, please reach a human being tonight rather than a page: in the UK,
        Samaritans on <strong>116&nbsp;123</strong>, free, any hour; elsewhere, your local
        emergency number or crisis line.</p>
        <p>The order&rsquo;s own standard applies to its own oracle. Test everything kindly. An
        answer that cannot survive your scepticism has not earned your belief, whoever &mdash; or
        whatever &mdash; wrote it.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

